//   * Both `traceroute` and `dig`/`nslookup`/`curl` ship with macOS
//     (/usr/sbin/traceroute, /usr/bin/nslookup, /usr/bin/dig, /usr/bin/curl).
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The recurring three-beat block: split the problem, look each part up, connect
// it to something already built. Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each
// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain. Every reference points BACKWARDS: 9.4, 9.3, 9.1, 9.7.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big: 'The page you are reading came to you across the internet. Between your finger and this sentence there is a name that had to become a number, a file that had to be cut into pieces, a path that no single machine knows, and a rule deciding whether a missing piece is worth waiting for. "The internet" is not one system. It is five, and each one can be checked from the machine in front of you.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Turn a name into a number',
      does: 'A name is for you; a router only moves numbers. The lookup walks a hierarchy — the root servers, then the `.io` servers, then GitHub\'s own — and each level keeps the answer it just gave so the next asker does not have to climb the whole tree.',
      doc: 'the DNS section of the Unit 9 Internet reference, and the guid-versus-filename trade you made in 9.4',
      href: '9.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Cut the message into pieces that each fit',
      does: 'A packet carries 1,460 bytes of your file once the 40 bytes of IP and TCP headers are subtracted. A file is never sent as a file: it is divided into as many pieces as the arithmetic demands, and the last piece is mostly empty.',
      doc: 'Step 4 of this lesson\'s Build Lab, and the size arithmetic you ran on pictures in 9.3',
      href: '9.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Address every piece so it can travel alone',
      does: 'Every packet repeats four facts in its own header — where it came from, where it is going, where it sits in the sequence, and a checksum. That repetition is what lets the pieces take different routes and still be put back in the right order.',
      doc: 'the header table in this lesson\'s Concept Lab, and the "Packets, not streams" section of the Unit 9 Internet reference',
    },
    {
      name: 'Let a machine with no map make one local decision',
      does: 'No router knows the path. Each one holds a table saying which neighbour is closer to a given network and hands the packet on, which is why two packets of the same file can cross different countries and still arrive.',
      doc: 'the routing-and-latency section of the Unit 9 Internet reference, and the trace you run in Step 3 of this lesson\'s Build Lab',
      href: '../docs/unit-09/the-internet.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide whether a missing piece is worth waiting for',
      does: 'TCP numbers every packet, waits for an acknowledgement and resends, so nothing is lost and nothing arrives out of order. UDP does none of that, which makes it faster and lossy — the right trade for a position that will be stale before it lands.',
      doc: 'the TCP-versus-UDP table in this lesson\'s Concept Lab, and the "TCP vs UDP" section of the Unit 9 Internet reference',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the whole lesson. "How does the internet work" is a question you can only answer by waving your hands. These five are five things you can settle from the machine in front of you: the first is a command you type and a hierarchy you watch answer, the second and the fourth are arithmetic on your own files, and the last is the tradeoff the exam asks about in every form it owns. Nothing here needs Unity open, because nothing here is a game — it is the network the game will one day have to talk over.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you settle the part from a terminal and a file listing? The lookup gives you four numbers back, the packet part gives you a division, and the routing part gives you a list of hops with milliseconds beside them. A part that needs a server of your own before you can check it is not a part of this problem — it is a project.',
  connect: [
    'In 9.4 a guid was the identity of a file and the filename was only a label on a folder listing. DNS is that same trade played on the whole planet: `aramansell.github.io` is the label and 185.199.108.153 is the identity. Which one did your browser open a connection to — and what did the name buy you that the number cannot?',
    'In 9.1 a signed 32-bit `int` stops at 2,147,483,647 and one step past it wraps to the most negative value. An IPv4 address is a 32-bit number too, and the address above is 3,116,854,425 written out. Which C# type holds that without overflowing, and what does your answer say about a width the internet chose before either of us was born?',
    'In 9.3 you turned a picture into bytes with `width × height × 4` and compared that to the size on disk. A packet is the same arithmetic run the other way: a fixed number of bytes per unit, and a division that tells you how many units. Your biggest scene file is 2,377,344 bytes and one packet carries 1,460 of them. How many packets is that, and why is the answer never a whole number?',
    'In 9.7 you learned four organs and what each one costs, and a network was not one of them. So when a push sits at "resolving deltas" for eight seconds before anything leaves the machine, which of those four organs is busy? Answer that first, and then say why reaching for the wifi is the wrong first instinct.',
  ],
  turns: [
    'The name this page is served under has four addresses and your browser used one of them. A classmate in the same room may have used a different one and still received the same bytes. What does that tell you about which machine served you — and what would have to change about the reply before the answer was different?',
    'One packet carries 1,460 bytes and your whole `Assets/` folder is 20,185,356 bytes over 4,967 files. Work out the packet count, then say what the very last packet carries that none of the others does, and why the count must always round up rather than down.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>9.8 — The Internet: Packets, Routing &amp; DNS</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The page you are reading crossed the internet to get here, and it did
    that before you finished the first sentence. Your browser turned the
    name <code>aramansell.github.io</code> into one of four numbers, cut
    this file into pieces small enough for the wire, and let machines that
    have never seen a map carry them. Today you do all of it on purpose,
    and read the answers back.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how does a message find its way across the internet?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.8', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Nothing crosses the internet whole.</strong> A packet
    carries at most <strong>1,460 bytes</strong> of your file. The limit is
    the MTU — the largest frame a link will carry, 1,500 bytes on Ethernet
    — and the IP and TCP headers take 40 of those bytes before your data
    gets any of them. So a file is never sent as a file. It is divided into
    as many 1,460-byte pieces as the arithmetic demands, and the last piece
    is mostly empty. Your biggest scene file is
    <code>Assets/_Scenes/Overworld.unity</code> at 2,377,344 bytes:
    <strong>1,629 packets</strong>, and the 1,629th carries the leftovers.</p>

    <p>Each packet carries its own header, repeated in full on every single
    one of them:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
        <tr><td>FROM address</td><td>Where the packet started, so the reply knows where to go.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TO address</td><td>Where it is going — a number, never a name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sequence number</td><td>Where this piece sits in the file, so order survives the trip.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Checksum</td><td>A sum of the packet's own bytes, so a garbled one is caught rather than assembled.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>That repetition is the design, not the overhead. Because every packet
    can be addressed and ordered on its own, the packets do not have to
    travel together — they can take different routes and still be put back
    in the right order at the far end. If one dies, only that one is resent,
    and the rest of the file never knows. This is what "the internet routes
    around damage" means: a design with no single point of failure.</p>

    <p><strong>TCP vs UDP — the reliability trade.</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>TCP</th><th>UDP</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Guarantee</td><td>All packets, in order, or none</td><td>Best effort; drops are fine</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Speed cost</td><td>Handshake, receipts, resends</td><td>Fire and forget</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Used for</td><td>Web pages, file transfer, email</td><td>Game packets, video calls, voice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your world</td><td>This page arrived whole and you never saw a gap</td><td>A position 10 ms late is DROPPED, never awaited</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>That split is the exam's favourite tradeoff: a game would rather LOSE
    a packet than WAIT for one, and a page reader would rather wait.</p>

    <p><strong>DNS — names to numbers.</strong>
    <code>aramansell.github.io</code> is a name for humans. Routers move
    numbers, and this name has four of them:</p>
    <pre><code>185.199.108.153   185.199.109.153   185.199.110.153   185.199.111.153</code></pre>
    <p>DNS is the lookup that converts, hierarchically, with caching at
    every level. Hierarchically, because no single machine on earth holds
    every name: the root servers know who runs <code>.io</code>, the
    <code>.io</code> servers know who runs <code>github.io</code>, and
    GitHub's nameservers know the four addresses. With caching, because
    climbing that tree costs about a tenth of a second, and repeating it
    for every image on every page would be unbearable — so every level
    keeps the answer it just gave, and hands it straight back next time.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The site you are reading is a GitHub Pages project
        site.</strong> <code>astro.config.mjs</code> sets
        <code>site: 'https://aramansell.github.io'</code> and
        <code>base: '/AP_CS_Principles'</code>, and there is no
        <code>CNAME</code> file anywhere in the repository — which is why
        the hostname is <code>aramansell.github.io</code> and not a domain
        the course owns. Ask that host for its certificate and it will not
        name this site back to you: it names <code>*.github.io</code>, one
        wildcard certificate covering every Pages site there is. All of
        that was checked live while this lesson was written, and all of it
        is two commands away from you in Step 1.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Your browser knows <code>aramansell.github.io</code> and
            connects to a number. Walk the lookup — your machine's cache,
            then the school's resolver, then the root servers, then the
            <code>.io</code> servers, then GitHub's own — and say where
            the hundred milliseconds go. The resolver's answer comes back
            marked <em>non-authoritative</em>, which means it did not ask
            anyone just now. What does that word promise you, and why is
            caching the difference between a page that loads and one that
            does not?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — resolve this site's own name.</strong> You are
    reading a page that had to be fetched by name. Ask what that name
    means, in Terminal:</p>
    <pre><code>nslookup aramansell.github.io</code></pre>
    <p>Read the answer twice. The first reading is the four addresses
    above, one of which — <code>185.199.108.153</code> — is the one
    <code>ping aramansell.github.io</code> picks out. The second reading is
    the line sitting above them: <strong>Non-authoritative answer</strong>.
    That phrase is the whole of caching in two words. Your machine asked
    its resolver, and the resolver answered from something it already
    remembered instead of going and asking. It is telling you, honestly,
    that it did not check.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — climb the hierarchy yourself.</strong> The cached
    answer is what you get in normal life. This is what it cost the first
    time, and it is the only way to see the shape:</p>
    <pre><code>dig +trace aramansell.github.io</code></pre>
    <p>Read it top to bottom. It starts at the root servers — thirteen of
    them, <code>a.root-servers.net</code> through
    <code>m.root-servers.net</code> — asks which machines run
    <code>.io</code>, asks those which machines run <code>github.io</code>,
    and asks those for the four addresses. Four questions, four different
    organisations, and not one of the machines involved knew the answer
    when it started. On the machine this lesson was written on, the climb
    cost 25 ms to a root server, 103 ms to the <code>.io</code> servers and
    7 ms for the final answer — against roughly 10 ms for the whole cached
    lookup in Step 1. <em>Your milliseconds will differ; the shape will
    not.</em> That gap is what caching buys, and it is why the first visit
    to a site is the slow one.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — see a route, and read the server's own
    header.</strong> Every line of a trace is one router making one local
    decision:</p>
    <pre><code>traceroute aramansell.github.io</code></pre>
    <p>The milliseconds jump where your traffic leaves one network and
    enters another — usually the boundary between your school and the open
    internet. Many school and hotel networks drop the probes
    <code>traceroute</code> sends, so a row of asterisks is a fact about
    the network rather than a broken command. Then ask the far end who it
    is:</p>
    <pre><code>curl -I https://aramansell.github.io/AP_CS_Principles/lessons/9.8.html</code></pre>
    <p>Among the headers is <code>server: GitHub.com</code> — the machine
    naming itself — and <code>cache-control: max-age=600</code>, which is
    GitHub telling your browser it may keep its copy for ten minutes before
    asking again. That is the same caching decision you just watched DNS
    make, written down as policy instead of left as a habit.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — price the trip in packets.</strong> One packet
    carries 1,460 bytes of your file, so a packet count is a division and a
    rounding up. Do these three on paper:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><code>Assets/_Scenes/Overworld.unity</code>, 2,377,344 bytes
        — <strong>1,629 packets</strong>.</li>
        <li>Your whole <code>Assets/</code> folder: 4,967 files,
        20,185,356 bytes. How many packets?</li>
        <li>The biggest picture in the project,
        <code>mainlevbuild.png</code>, 654,657 bytes. How many packets,
        and how much of the last one is actually used?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Not one of those answers is a whole number of anything, and the
    leftover on the final packet is why the count always rounds up instead
    of down. It is the same arithmetic you ran on pictures in
    <a href="9.3.html">9.3</a>, pointed the other way.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Network failure modes</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your push sits at "resolving deltas" for eight seconds
                before a single byte leaves the machine. A delta is a
                difference between two versions of the same object,
                computed on YOUR side. Which of the four organs from
                <a href="9.7.html">9.7</a> is busy — and what does that
                say about blaming the wifi when a push feels slow?</li>
            <li>You mean to type <code>github.io</code> and your fingers
                produce <code>gihub.io</code>. Ask DNS about that name: it
                answers, with an address nobody at GitHub owns. Why is
                "the name resolved" not the same claim as "you reached
                the right machine?"</li>
            <li>Your push works on school wifi and times out at home.
                Which TWO layers do you check first — DNS, your router,
                your ISP, or GitHub — and which command settles the
                question? Remember that a row of asterisks from
                <code>traceroute</code> is an answer too.</li>
            <li>The exam classic: why is UDP appropriate for a
                multiplayer game but TCP appropriate for downloading the
                game? Answer in one sentence each with the drop-versus-wait
                trade, then say which one you would choose for the final
                score a run posts, and why that is not the same question
                as the positions sent while the run is happening.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design for the network</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your platformer has two turrets in the Overworld and no
                network code at all. Design the messages a two-player
                version would need — list five, and for each one say
                whether it is UDP-worthy (an old position is worthless by
                the time it lands) or TCP-worthy (a result that must
                arrive exactly once). Which of your five would you cut
                first if the connection got bad?</li>
            <li>Making the player wait for the server before every jump
                would make the game feel broken. What is the lag-tolerant
                alternative, and why does letting the player move NOW and
                correcting later feel instant even at 80 ms? Name the one
                thing that has to be true of the correction for it to be
                fair.</li>
            <li>Hard: your game's leaderboard (someday) posts a score of
                999999. What must the SERVER do before it believes that
                number, and why is "the client can lie" the single
                sentence of network security? (<a href="9.9.html">9.9</a>
                bridges here.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain packets, headers, sequence numbers, and reassembly</li>
            <li>Choose TCP vs UDP for a given application with reasons</li>
            <li>Resolve a real name and read the answer, cached and uncached</li>
            <li>Read the DNS hierarchy out of a <code>+trace</code> and say what each level knows</li>
            <li>Turn a file size into a packet count, and explain the leftover</li>
            <li>Tell DNS, the network and the server apart when something fails</li>
            <li>Break this topic into parts small enough to check each one on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
            <li>Say which earlier lesson each part came from, and where you would reread it</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/the-internet.html">The Internet</a> — the full reference: packets, TCP vs UDP, DNS and routing</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/index.html">Unit 9: Data &amp; Systems</a> — networks in the CED</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="9.9.html">9.9 — Encryption: Keys, TLS &amp; Public Key</a></p>
</div>`;
